docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:34:**WEIS: 3/5, upper borderline. AAMAS main track: 2/5. ACM EC: 1/5.** The numerical core survives this review. The churn and reset analyses reproduce, and most round-5 corrections are now present. The paper has a clear, defensible result: under the prescribed searches, R3’s advantage in markets without honest identity turnover deteriorates when honest providers also replace identities; its comparisons with C* and B3 deteriorate differently. The revision now reports those differences with appropriate qualifications. The remaining weakness is the reach of the result: one market neighbourhood, one verified-history comparator under churn, fixed audit parameters, restricted attackers, and unresolved search error. I found several factual and scope errors that should be corrected, but none overturns the main churn tables. The next substantial improvement should be a stronger scientific test or a result that explains how the effect changes with audit capacity and turnover. Another cycle of small wording repairs alone will not materially change the venue assessment. Sources: paper §§8–11; reproduced C, M and reset outputs.
