docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:346:The paper formally distinguishes a missing or failed execution from a completed search flagged by its doubling check.
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:350:> “the frozen value includes one search-flagged cell with a large improvement under doubling”.
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:361:Add the existing newest-identity frequencies, with their denominator defined, and change the response to say that only the zero-churn allocation baseline still needs a diagnostics pass.
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:363:For release preparation, also repair the hard-coded test path to `runnerup_analysis.json` and include the Run A raw records needed to replay its intervals. These are reproducibility improvements; the missing records do not demonstrate that the reported result is wrong.
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:371:> “The churn reversals therefore have narrow seed-bootstrap intervals on these six markets … and they are not established beyond these markets.” (p.15).
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:373:That concerns the **absolute ordering at each churn rate**. A separate question is whether churn reduces R3’s advantage relative to the same market without churn.
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:375:I paired each market’s contrast with its own no-churn contrast:
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:382:Here \(d=C^*-R3\) and \(q=B3-R3\). Negative changes mean that R3’s relative position deteriorates. Each version uses its own no-churn baseline.
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:397:At **0.03 and 0.05, both contrasts deteriorate on every sampled market under both search versions**. That is a clearer descriptive result than a general claim that the ranking reverses.
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:399:These intervals are post hoc, unadjusted and based on few markets. They omit search uncertainty, and the nominal exclusions of zero should not be promoted into a new confirmatory claim. The complete rate table should be reported if this analysis is added.
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:403:> “The market-level intervals above concern the absolute ordering at each churn rate. In an exploratory paired comparison with each market’s no-churn result, both contrasts decreased on every sampled market at p = 0.03 and p = 0.05 under both search versions. This supports deterioration of R3’s measured relative performance, while the population ordering under churn remains uncertain.”
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:411:| **WEIS 2027** | **3/5, upper borderline** | **50%** | Medium | A prospectively specified churn replication on new market draws, including both C* and the runner-up, with sufficient continued search to assess the cells that determine the result. |
